{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to the AI Act, what are the main obligations for providers of high-risk AI systems, and how must compliance be demonstrated?", "answer": "Providers of high-risk AI systems must ensure risk management, quality data, technical documentation, logging, transparency, human oversight, and cybersecurity, with compliance shown through conformity assessments.", "evidence": [{"page": 16, "quote": "- Establishment, application, documentation and maintenance of a risk management system, Article 9 [10];

- Development and training of high-risk systems with training and validation data according to certain quality criteria, data governance, Article 10 [10];

- Technical documentation, Article 11 [10];

- Automatic recording of processes and events (logging) according to recognized standards, Article 12 [10];

- Transparency and provision of information to the deployer, Article 13 [10];

- Enabling human oversight during the use of the system, Article 14 [10];

- Accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity in relation to the intended purpose of the specific system, Article 15 [10]."}, {"page": 16, "quote": "Compliance with the safety requirements must be demonstrated by the provider through a conformity assessment procedure. 57 In most cases this procedure will take place as part of an internal control. This is particularly the case if a system is considered a high-risk system within the meaning of Annex III [10] and available technical standards have been applied completely. 58 All other high-risk systems must undergo an external assessment procedure under the above-mentioned harmonized acts, which will in future also verify compliance with the safety requirements of the AI Act. 59"}]},
  {"question": "How does the AI Act address the obligations of GPAI model providers when their models are integrated into high-risk SPAI systems, and what challenges arise for downstream providers?", "answer": "GPAI model providers must supply information to downstream providers, but this is often minimal due to IP concerns, making it hard for downstream providers to verify compliance, especially regarding training data quality.", "evidence": [{"page": 19, "quote": "As outlined above, the provider of GPAI models, in our case SmartLLM, has certain information obligations for the benefit of downstream providers who build an AI system with a specific purpose based on the GPAI model. 75 The provision of this information also is intended to make it easier for providers of downstream systems to comply with the obligations of the AI Act, 76 which in our setting is the provider of AutoRa. However, the information obligations shall apply without prejudice to the provider's intellectual property and trade secrets. 77 Accordingly, the majority of providers of GPAI models will reduce the provided information to a minimum, thereby hampering verification of compliance through downstream providers."}, {"page": 21, "quote": "To our understanding, the AI Act obliges the downstream system providers to ensure that the high-risk requirements are met, including with respect to the underlying GPAI model. This may pose a major hurdle for the downstream provider when using a third party's GPAI model."} ]},
  {"question": "What is the definition of 'safety component' in the context of high-risk AI systems under the AI Act?", "answer": "A 'safety component' is a component of a product or AI system that fulfills a safety function or whose failure endangers health and safety.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": ""Safety components" are defined in Article 3(14) [10] as a component of a product or of an AI system which fulfils a safety function for that product or AI system, or the failure or malfunctioning of which endangers the health and safety of persons or property."}]},
  {"question": "According to the AI Act, what are the transparency requirements for generative AI outputs?", "answer": "Generative AI outputs must be labelled in a machine-readable format to identify them as artificially generated or manipulated, unless only minor changes are made to input data.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "The providers of these models must ensure that the output of their systems is labelled in a machine-readable format and can be identified as artificially generated or manipulated. 70 This obligation does not apply if the system or model makes minor changes to the input data."}]},
  {"question": "When does the AI Act require Member States to establish authorities and notification procedures for GPAI models with systemic risks?", "answer": "Within 12 months of entry into force, Member States must set up authorities and notification procedures for GPAI models presenting systemic risks.", "evidence": [{"page": 23, "quote": "Within 12 months, the provisions requiring Member States to set up authorities as well as procedures on notification of general-purpose AI models presenting systemic risks become applicable (Article 51 [10]). 87"}]},
  {"question": "Which types of AI systems are banned under Article 5 of the AI Act due to unacceptable risks?", "answer": "AI systems that use subliminal techniques causing harm, exploit vulnerabilities, use social scoring, predict criminal behavior solely by profiling, scrape facial images, infer emotions outside medical/safety contexts, or categorize by biometrics are banned.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": "Article 5 [10] prohibits AI systems with unacceptable risks that cause significant harm by distorting behavior, exploiting vulnerabilities, using social scoring, predicting criminal behavior based solely on profiling, scraping facial images, inferring emotions outside of medical or security contexts, or categorizing individuals based on biometrics, while also restricting real-time biometric identification in public spaces with exceptions."}]}
]}
